{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE PROVEN — experiment 30981084637\n\n    [before]            HTTP 200 total=27 customfield_count=0   EpicLink=False EpicName=False\n    [after-180s-quiet]  HTTP 200 total=27 customfield_count=0   EpicLink=False EpicName=False\n    create project -> HTTP 201 {'id': 10000, 'key': 'RBJEXPT'}\n    [after-create+0s]   HTTP 200 total=55 customfield_count=13  EpicLink=True  EpicName=True\n    VERDICT: before_create_ready=False after_180s_quiet_ready=False\n             after_create_ready=True elapsed_after_create=0.0512\n\n**180 seconds of additional quiet time moved NOTHING** (27 fields, zero `customfield_*`, both\nEpic fields absent). **Creating the pinned GreenHopper scrum project moved everything within\n0.05 seconds** (27 -> 55 fields, 0 -> 13 `customfield_*`, both Epic fields present).\n\nThe quiet-settle leg is what makes this discriminating: it supplied TIME without the ACTION,\nand time did nothing. Any budget hypothesis is dead.\n\n### The proven mechanism\n\n`Epic Link`/`Epic Name` are provisioned by GreenHopper **when the first Jira Software project\nis created**, not at plugin startup. 9790's session-start gate therefore waits for a capability\nthat only the action it blocks can create. It is a **DEADLOCK**, and it is budget-independent —\nwhich is why 600s and 1800s produced byte-identical inventories.\n\nEvery symptom is explained by this one cause:\n- 30964805133 green pre-1406: no gate -> projects created -> fields provisioned ->\n  `_assert_project_capabilities` (which runs AFTER creation) passed.\n- 30975323866 (600s) and 30978613228 (1800s) red, identical inventories, idle container.\n- probe runs 30944211742 and 30930839323 red with `Epic Link=None Epic Name=None`: the probe\n  has the SAME ordering bug — `_await_named_fields` at `jira_dc_epic_link_clear_probe.py:156`\n  runs before the project create at :168. Change 1387 'fixed the probe path' and the probe has\n  never passed since.\n- `docs/jira-dc-capability-map.md` records `Epic Link = customfield_10001`,\n  `Epic Name = customfield_10003` — the map agent had created a project first.\n\n### The most damaging artifact is the decision rule itself\n\n`not_ready_message` tells the reader: *'if it contains no `customfield_*` entries whatsoever\nthe plugin is still starting and the budget (JIRA_DC_FIELD_READY_TIMEOUT) is too short'*. That\nrule is **wrong**, and it is self-confirming: an empty custom-field inventory is the NORMAL\nstate of a Jira Software instance with no project, so the message points every reader at the\nbudget. It misdiagnosed 9790, it misdiagnosed this ticket's own opening analysis, and it cost a\n50-minute run. Correcting that sentence is part of the fix, not a nicety.\n\n### Consequence for the fix\n\nThe budget was never the defect, so raising it is not the fix and 1800s will be reverted. The\ncapability check moves to the only place it can be answered — after project provisioning, in\n`_assert_project_capabilities`, which already exists and already asks exactly this question. Its\ncurrent 15s cap (`_CAPABILITY_FIELD_CONFIRM_BUDGET_S`) rests on a premise that is now false\n('readiness already waited out the full field budget at session start'), so that cap comes off\ntoo. The probe gets the same reordering."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785911802023361001,"uuid":"0b12d8d7-22fa-4fff-8aea-af4914ad2627"}